WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:02.601
-Come on. Welcome to the show.
You look fantastic.

00:00:02.601 --> 00:00:04.067
-Thank you bud,
thank you, thank you.

00:00:04.067 --> 00:00:07.033
I couldn't disappoint you.
-No, you never disappoint me.

00:00:07.033 --> 00:00:08.701
Tell me about this, though.

00:00:08.701 --> 00:00:11.400
You explain it a little bit
in "Relentless."

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.334
But congrats on the new book
here, "W1nning," first of all.

00:00:14.334 --> 00:00:16.067
But you started --

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.200
You didn't start
as a trainer or you did?

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.767
-Well, here's the thing.
I did start as a trainer.

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:21.234
So when I went to college,

00:00:21.234 --> 00:00:22.934
I didn't know what I wanted
to do, like everybody else.

00:00:22.934 --> 00:00:25.133
So kinesiology
was just getting started.

00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:27.000
-Your parents
were fine with this?

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:29.667
-No, they weren't, not at all.
-No.

00:00:29.667 --> 00:00:32.033
-Both my parents
come from India.

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.534
-Okay.
-Alright?

00:00:33.534 --> 00:00:37.834
So back in the '80s,
as a son of Indian parents,

00:00:37.834 --> 00:00:40.801
you got two options
for a profession.

00:00:40.801 --> 00:00:43.100
Doctor being one,
a doctor being the other.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:44.367
[ Laughter ]

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:45.734
That's it.

00:00:45.734 --> 00:00:47.801
I told them I wanted
to train professional athletes.

00:00:47.801 --> 00:00:49.434
They goes,
"What does that even mean?"

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:50.734
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:52.767
You get vacation time,
you get a two-week paycheck.

00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:55.734
I said, no, I said, I'm going
to train professional athletes.

00:00:55.734 --> 00:00:59.334
And, you know, behold,
everything I went through.

00:00:59.334 --> 00:01:01.567
And it ended up happening.
-Yeah. And you just did it.

00:01:01.567 --> 00:01:03.200
But then you get in there

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:04.968
and you have the opportunity
or you just said,

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:08.067
"I'm going to train
someone from the Bulls"?

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.767
-I was working
at a local health club.

00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:13.267
I was making $3.35 an hour
back in the '80s.

00:01:13.267 --> 00:01:15.400
That was my minimum wage.
I know yours was 7.50.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:18.701
I did some homework on you now.
-Wow, yeah, I did. I got 7.50.

00:01:18.701 --> 00:01:21.667
7.50.
-Yeah, yeah, I paid attention.

00:01:21.667 --> 00:01:23.100
-I started as soon as
I could start working.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:25.267
-That's exactly what I did.
You know, you sit --

00:01:25.267 --> 00:01:26.667
you can't be sitting at home,
you finish school,

00:01:26.667 --> 00:01:28.334
you're like, all right,
I've got to do something.

00:01:28.334 --> 00:01:31.200
So I took a job at a local
health club,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:32.367
stayed there
for about three years.

00:01:32.367 --> 00:01:34.067
I saw a small article
in the newspaper.

00:01:34.067 --> 00:01:36.234
It said Michael Jordan is tired
of taking a physical abuse

00:01:36.234 --> 00:01:40.167
from the Detroit Pistons.
Hmm. Light-bulb moment.

00:01:40.167 --> 00:01:43.734
So back then, remember,
no cellphones,

00:01:43.734 --> 00:01:45.834
no texting, no nothing.
-No.

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:49.033
-Handwrote 14 letters.

00:01:49.033 --> 00:01:52.934
15 players on the team.
Did not write to Michael Jordan.

00:01:52.934 --> 00:01:55.767
He's the best. I've never worked
with a professional athlete.

00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.634
-He'll never --
-Nah.

00:01:57.634 --> 00:02:00.501
There's just no way.
He's not -- No way.

00:02:00.501 --> 00:02:02.767
One person.

00:02:02.767 --> 00:02:04.701
He found the letter
in somebody else's locker,

00:02:04.701 --> 00:02:06.000
gave it to the athletic trainer
and said,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:07.801
"Hey, find out
what this kid's about."

00:02:07.801 --> 00:02:10.400
Lesson learned.
The best of the best always are

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.267
looking to get better.
Always looking to get better.

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:15.534
-This is you training
Michael Jordan.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:16.834
-Yeah, with the mustache.

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:18.634
That's the only picture
you can find, huh?

00:02:18.634 --> 00:02:19.801
[ Laughter ]

00:02:19.801 --> 00:02:22.000
-You look great.
You look fantastic.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.634
-That's the only picture
you can find.

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:25.601
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:25.601 --> 00:02:30.033
-But, I mean, this is
at his peak here with the Bulls.

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:32.000
What were these
training sessions like?

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:34.267
-Very, very intense.
Very intense.

00:02:34.267 --> 00:02:37.834
It would be either 5 a.m.
in the morning, 6 a.m. or 7.

00:02:37.834 --> 00:02:41.434
That was our time that we wanted
to get the workouts in,

00:02:41.434 --> 00:02:44.801
and they were very intense
and I was doing stuff back --

00:02:44.801 --> 00:02:47.834
we're talking about '89, '90.

00:02:47.834 --> 00:02:50.868
I was doing back stuff where
people are just discovering now.

00:02:50.868 --> 00:02:53.400
I mean, we did the crazy --
we did the craziest things.

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.968
I did stuff for his fingers.
I did stuff for his wrists.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:57.801
I did stuff for his ankles.
-His fingers?

00:02:57.801 --> 00:02:58.868
-Yes, because in basketball,

00:02:58.868 --> 00:03:00.901
your fingers are constantly
jammed.

00:03:00.901 --> 00:03:02.033
You have to have the dexterity.

00:03:02.033 --> 00:03:03.267
You know, if you're
playing a piano,

00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:04.934
that's how you control
the basketball.

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:07.234
So different things
that weren't taught in school,

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:08.834
but I just knew were right.

00:03:08.834 --> 00:03:10.067
You know, you got
to trust your instincts.

00:03:10.067 --> 00:03:11.200
You just got to know, hey,

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.267
this is the right way
to do things.

00:03:13.267 --> 00:03:15.534
So, you know,
we'd have sometimes --

00:03:15.534 --> 00:03:18.834
I would literally go home
after a game.

00:03:18.834 --> 00:03:21.167
And this crowd may be
a little bit too young

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:22.601
to understand this,

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:25.267
but you had to record a game
on a machine called a Betamax.

00:03:25.267 --> 00:03:28.267
[ Laughter ]
Anybody know what a Betamax is?

00:03:28.267 --> 00:03:29.267
Alright.

00:03:29.267 --> 00:03:31.501
-We'll explain
what a Betamax is.

00:03:31.501 --> 00:03:33.701
Wow. On Beta?
-Betamax.

00:03:33.701 --> 00:03:35.734
-You made that investment?
-Yes, I had to.

00:03:35.734 --> 00:03:37.167
Yeah, it was a bad one.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:37.167 --> 00:03:39.334
-It was the only investment,
bad one you made.

00:03:39.334 --> 00:03:41.133
-Yeah, it was bad. It was bad.

00:03:41.133 --> 00:03:42.801
-But I would go back home

00:03:42.801 --> 00:03:45.067
and we'd be the last ones to
leave the stadium and so forth.

00:03:45.067 --> 00:03:46.400
Because I never worked
for the Bulls.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:48.400
I actually worked
directly for him.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.667
So I'd go back home

00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:51.801
and I would literally watch
the game over again.

00:03:51.801 --> 00:03:55.667
Not for the highlights.
To count his steps.

00:03:55.667 --> 00:03:58.334
-You actually counted how
many steps he took on the court?

00:03:58.334 --> 00:04:00.567
-How much -- how many steps
he took left,

00:04:00.567 --> 00:04:01.834
how many steps he took right,

00:04:01.834 --> 00:04:03.767
how many times
he landed on one foot,

00:04:03.767 --> 00:04:05.501
how many landed
on the other foot.

00:04:05.501 --> 00:04:07.000
All these different things

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.901
because I was like, okay, well,
if one side is being used more

00:04:09.901 --> 00:04:12.300
than the other, well, we can't
train them the same way.

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:13.634
We can't train them
the same way.

00:04:13.634 --> 00:04:15.234
So I would hand him weights
and one hand

00:04:15.234 --> 00:04:17.434
would be completely different
than the weights on the other.

00:04:17.434 --> 00:04:19.367
The reps on one side would be
completely different

00:04:19.367 --> 00:04:21.133
than the other,
just different things.

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:22.501
We did stuff for his eyes.

00:04:22.501 --> 00:04:24.734
We created all kinds
of distractions.

00:04:24.734 --> 00:04:27.067
Just stuff that I knew
that would work,

00:04:27.067 --> 00:04:30.000
that would just work.
And he was open to it.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.467
He was like open because
he was just like, "Man,

00:04:32.467 --> 00:04:34.467
just -- I don't want to do
what everybody else is doing.

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:36.133
If I do what
everybody else is doing,

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.200
I'm going to be like
everybody else."

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:40.033
-Yeah. And he
just wanted to win.

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:41.901
He's like, "I'm going to."

00:04:41.901 --> 00:04:44.400
-His obsession with winning.
-Yeah.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.667
-He knew there was a price
that needed to pay for winning.

00:04:47.667 --> 00:04:49.834
And he was -- Most people --
Everybody knows

00:04:49.834 --> 00:04:51.834
you got to pay
a price for winning,

00:04:51.834 --> 00:04:54.234
but most people
aren't willing to pay it.

00:04:54.234 --> 00:04:55.667
They may pay it once,

00:04:55.667 --> 00:04:58.234
but literally you got
to pay it every single day.

00:04:58.234 --> 00:05:02.434
And the crazy part about that
is the price changes daily,

00:05:02.434 --> 00:05:04.234
just like the stock market
goes up and down.

00:05:04.234 --> 00:05:08.267
The price of winning changes
on a daily, daily basis.

00:05:08.267 --> 00:05:10.067
And you got to be willing
to pay that price,

00:05:10.067 --> 00:05:12.000
whatever it is on that day.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.434
-Is that why,
like, all of your --

00:05:14.434 --> 00:05:16.567
You make points, almost like a,
I don't want to

00:05:16.567 --> 00:05:18.801
say it's a 10-step program
or something like that.

00:05:18.801 --> 00:05:22.167
But usually you see the 10 steps
on becoming successful.

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:26.100
The 10 steps -- All of your
steps are named number one.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.667
-Yes.
-Number one, do this.

00:05:28.667 --> 00:05:29.801
Number one, do that.

00:05:29.801 --> 00:05:31.834
Number one, do this.
Number one.

00:05:31.834 --> 00:05:34.467
They're all number one.
-Right.

00:05:34.467 --> 00:05:35.701
-Why?
-Not a typo.

00:05:35.701 --> 00:05:37.400
-No.
-Not a typo.

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.601
So here's the thing.
With my ideas is --

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:42.901
and the audience can relate
to this -- when you get a list,

00:05:42.901 --> 00:05:44.601
you see things one,
two, three, four.

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:48.400
People pay attention to one,
not as much to two, eight.

00:05:48.400 --> 00:05:50.200
When you get down to 11, 12,
they're like,

00:05:50.200 --> 00:05:51.734
eh, it doesn't matter.
-Yeah.

00:05:51.734 --> 00:05:54.667
-It doesn't matter. If you
list everything as number one,

00:05:54.667 --> 00:05:57.934
and I knew all of these
were equally as important.

00:05:57.934 --> 00:06:00.434
But what was number one, what
was the most important thing,

00:06:00.434 --> 00:06:03.634
what your number one may not be
somebody else's number one.

00:06:03.634 --> 00:06:06.367
So you can take that list
and interchange it

00:06:06.367 --> 00:06:10.400
to your definition
and your vision of winning

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.400
and your vision
of relentlessness, take it

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:13.667
and just like,
hey, you know what?

00:06:13.667 --> 00:06:16.734
Today I need this
to be my number one.

00:06:16.734 --> 00:06:18.100
-Yeah.
-But that doesn't mean

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:20.934
this -- doesn't mean
discount the other stuff.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:23.667
-I was so inspired
by "Relentless."

00:06:23.667 --> 00:06:25.267
I loved it so much.

00:06:25.267 --> 00:06:27.133
Do you have people
coming up to you

00:06:27.133 --> 00:06:29.934
and saying like, "Wow, this book
changed my life"?

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:31.667
-I have people coming to me
all the time to say

00:06:31.667 --> 00:06:33.434
"the book changed my life" and,
you know what I do is,

00:06:33.434 --> 00:06:35.834
I didn't -- the book
didn't change your life.

00:06:35.834 --> 00:06:38.868
You changed your life. The book
just gave you permission.

00:06:38.868 --> 00:06:41.100
It gave you permission.
You know what "Relentless" did

00:06:41.100 --> 00:06:43.734
to a lot of individuals?

00:06:43.734 --> 00:06:46.567
It gave them -- They understood
they're not alone.

00:06:46.567 --> 00:06:49.067
They were not alone,
that this mentality --

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:50.734
Everyone thought, "Man,
I thought it was the only one.

00:06:50.734 --> 00:06:52.234
I thought I was the only one."

00:06:52.234 --> 00:06:54.767
And when they read the book,
they were like, "Wow,

00:06:54.767 --> 00:06:56.267
there's a lot of us like this.

00:06:56.267 --> 00:06:59.033
There's a lot of us
that have that same mentality."

00:06:59.033 --> 00:07:00.467
-I read the book and then

00:07:00.467 --> 00:07:02.367
I also got the audio book,
by the way.

00:07:02.367 --> 00:07:04.801
And who helped you
with the book as well?

00:07:04.801 --> 00:07:08.501
-My agent, my business partner
and my coauthor, Shari Wenk.

00:07:08.501 --> 00:07:10.501
-Shari Wenk, who I met
backstage. She's fantastic.

00:07:10.501 --> 00:07:13.167
-She is fantastic. She's one
of the reasons I'm here, folks.

00:07:13.167 --> 00:07:14.267
Give it up for her,
will you, please?

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.567
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:16.567 --> 00:07:17.901
-She really is great.

00:07:17.901 --> 00:07:20.834
And if you like the book,
you get the audio book

00:07:20.834 --> 00:07:22.968
and it's extra stuff and you
guys having conversations

00:07:22.968 --> 00:07:24.934
and then -- so there's
little bonus stuff.

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:26.133
But I did that as well.

00:07:26.133 --> 00:07:27.767
And whoever you got
to read the audio book,

00:07:27.767 --> 00:07:28.968
by the way, was fantastic.

00:07:28.968 --> 00:07:31.100
And his voice is
very intimidating.

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:34.067
And I was like, "Wow, this was
really great." I loved it.

00:07:34.067 --> 00:07:36.167
Can we talk a little bit
about Kobe Bryant?

00:07:36.167 --> 00:07:37.367
-Yes, we can.

00:07:37.367 --> 00:07:38.901
-Here's, you, no mustache,
with Kobe.

00:07:38.901 --> 00:07:42.067
-Yes. A suit that didn't fit.
But back then that was --

00:07:42.067 --> 00:07:44.200
well, maybe wasn't a style
but that back then

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.067
that's what I wore.
-Yeah.

00:07:45.067 --> 00:07:46.434
[ Laughter ]

00:07:46.434 --> 00:07:51.133
-What can we say about Kobe,
his style of training?

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:54.067
-I mean, he's just
the definition of winning, man.

00:07:54.067 --> 00:07:55.367
It was unbelievable.

00:07:55.367 --> 00:07:57.367
You know, when you ask
individuals -- It was funny.

00:07:57.367 --> 00:07:59.000
I asked all my clients,
including him.

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:02.300
I said, "Listen,
describe winning in one word."

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:04.367
And, you know, you get a lot
of individuals that tell you,

00:08:04.367 --> 00:08:07.167
you know, it's joyful,
it's happiness, it's fun.

00:08:07.167 --> 00:08:08.567
It's all these other things.

00:08:08.567 --> 00:08:10.934
But the true winners,
that's the end of winning.

00:08:10.934 --> 00:08:13.601
That's at the end of winning.

00:08:13.601 --> 00:08:15.300
But when you have
to really describe winning,

00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:17.167
what it really means
and what it takes,

00:08:17.167 --> 00:08:19.100
it's, you know,
it's unapologetic,

00:08:19.100 --> 00:08:21.067
it's dirty, it's nasty.

00:08:21.067 --> 00:08:23.467
If you go through
all those things,

00:08:23.467 --> 00:08:25.334
then you get to the joy part

00:08:25.334 --> 00:08:28.234
and then you get to the end.

00:08:28.234 --> 00:08:30.200
But it's all the obstacles
in between.

00:08:30.200 --> 00:08:33.434
It's about the grind and knowing
you're grinding for a purpose.

00:08:33.434 --> 00:08:35.100
You know, individuals
always say, "Listen,

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.400
yeah,
I'm grinding, I'm grinding."

00:08:36.400 --> 00:08:38.133
Everybody said, "Man,
I'm grinding for success.

00:08:38.133 --> 00:08:39.367
I'm grinding for this."

00:08:39.367 --> 00:08:41.033
Well, if you take
two pieces of object

00:08:41.033 --> 00:08:42.901
and you just grind them
together, what happens?

00:08:42.901 --> 00:08:44.300
It turns into dust.
-Yeah.

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:46.801
-But when you see people
do ice sculptures

00:08:46.801 --> 00:08:49.100
or you see clay or art
or, you know,

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:52.501
if we have any
artistic individuals out here,

00:08:52.501 --> 00:08:54.234
they grind away
the unessentials

00:08:54.234 --> 00:08:55.767
to form what they need,

00:08:55.767 --> 00:08:57.367
what that piece
is going to look like.

00:08:57.367 --> 00:08:59.167
And that's what Kobe did.
That's what Kobe did.

00:08:59.167 --> 00:09:01.133
That's what Michael did.
That's what Dwyane did.

00:09:01.133 --> 00:09:02.734
That's what
all these individuals do,

00:09:02.734 --> 00:09:04.801
is they grind away
the unessentials

00:09:04.801 --> 00:09:06.334
so they can have this sculpture

00:09:06.334 --> 00:09:07.934
of what winning
looks like for them,

00:09:07.934 --> 00:09:09.934
and it looks different
for each individual.

00:09:09.934 --> 00:09:12.734
As slight as it may be,
it's different.

00:09:12.734 --> 00:09:15.467
-Yeah.
-Just a little bit different.

00:09:15.467 --> 00:09:17.634
-I can't even tell you.
This is exactly what you read.

00:09:17.634 --> 00:09:19.801
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:19.801 --> 00:09:22.868
This book has helped
a lot of people,

00:09:22.868 --> 00:09:24.400
and you were nice enough

00:09:24.400 --> 00:09:26.367
to bring a copy of "W1nning"
for everyone

00:09:26.367 --> 00:09:28.434
in our audience tonight.
Come on!

00:09:28.434 --> 00:09:30.901
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:09:30.901 --> 00:09:32.601
I knew you'd be great.

00:09:32.601 --> 00:09:33.667
Come on.

